<section id="faq" class="sec"><div class="wrap faq">
  <div class="head"><h2 class="sub">FAQs</h2><h3 class="big">Questions people <em>ask</em>.</h3><div class="r"></div></div>
  <div>
<details open><summary>Who do you work with?</summary><p>First-time buyers, repeat buyers, relocators and sellers.</p></details>
<details><summary>Do you only work in San Francisco?</summary><p>Primarily, yes. Real estate works hyper-locally, and San Francisco is the market I know best. Some agents try to cover the whole Bay Area, and I don&#x27;t. I know the East Bay well and will work there for special clients and my own circle. For other parts of the Bay, the state and the country, I have referral partners I trust, so you get the best of their market too.</p></details>
<details><summary>Are you new to real estate, and does that matter for me?</summary><p>I&#x27;m newer as an agent, but real estate isn&#x27;t new to me: my first job was helping manage my grandmother&#x27;s properties. I focus on quality of service over volume, so I bring a hunger to learn and a fresh approach to marketing, presentation and the technology that makes the process smoother.</p></details>
<details><summary>What does education-first mean?</summary><p>It means I&#x27;d rather teach you how San Francisco real estate works than rush you into a decision. Before you buy or sell, we go through the numbers, the process and the trade-offs in plain English, so you understand every step and can make the call that&#x27;s right for your life and your long-term finances.</p></details>
<details><summary>Why do you call yourself an insider?</summary><p>Because I want you to see how San Francisco real estate really works. I explain buying and selling in plain English, share what I know about the city, and act as your advocate from inside the industry.</p></details>
<details><summary>I&#x27;m not ready to buy yet. Is it okay to reach out?</summary><p>Yes, please do. Trust builds over years, not transactions, and the best buyers are the most prepared. Time does matter in real estate, but rushing into a decision you weren&#x27;t ready for often leads to regret. I&#x27;d rather help you prepare for the long term and learn before you&#x27;re ready. Financial literacy matters so much, so reach out early.</p></details>
<details><summary>What&#x27;s the difference between the discovery call and the consult?</summary><p>The 20-minute discovery call is about connection and fit: your priorities, and whether I&#x27;m the right agent for you. There&#x27;s a saying that there&#x27;s a lid for every pot, and I&#x27;d rather we find yours, even if it isn&#x27;t me. We&#x27;re choosing each other, so think of it as a first date. The 60-minute consult is where we build trust and a game plan tailored to you.</p></details>
<details><summary>How do you help me figure out what I can afford?</summary><p>We start with your numbers, using my calculator and a &quot;what&#x27;s left to live on&quot; approach that shows what remains each month after housing and taxes. Then we look at your financial priorities. Maybe you&#x27;d buy below your means to keep room for spending. Maybe you&#x27;re committed to a specific home and willing to adjust your lifestyle for it. My job is to align your financial goals with your real estate goals.</p></details>
<details><summary>What happens after we close?</summary><p>Closing is the start of the relationship, not the end. I stay part of your life long after.</p></details>
  </div>
</div></section>

